NUMERICAL SIMULATION OF ICING ON THE ROTATING BLADE
Wei Dong
School of Mechanical Engineering Shanghai Jiao Tong University Shanghai, China
JianJun Zhu
School of Mechanical Engineering Shanghai Jiao Tong University Shanghai, China
Rui Wang
School of Mechanical Engineering Shanghai Jiao Tong University Shanghai, China
Yong Chen
School of Mechanical Engineering Shanghai Jiao Tong University Shanghai, China
ABSTRACT
The physical processes involved in ice accretion on the rotating blade are complex. It is important to develop high fidelity numerical method and simulate the icing process on the blade under icing conditions. This paper presents a numerical study on the icing process on the rotating blade. The flow field around the blade is obtained using ANSYS FLUENT. The trajectories of supercooled water droplets and the collection efficiency are calculated by Eulerian approach. Heat and mass balance on the rotating blade surface is taken into account in icing process simulations. The NASA Rotor 67 blade is chosen as the computational model. The collection efficiency on the blade surface is computed and the impingement characteristics are analyzed. The 3D icing accretion on Rotor 67 blade is predicted at design point. The ice shapes of accretion time of 5s, 10s and 15s are simulated and the ice shapes at different span positions of the rotating blade are compared.
INTRODUCTION
Ice accretion on the leading edge of the aircraft wing could adversely affect their aerodynamic characteristics and hence the performance. Ice accretion on the inlet of an aero-engine could also affect the characteristics of the inlet flow field and degrade the engine’s performance such as increasing fuel consumption, reducing airflow rate. Ice shed from the spinner or rotor can result in serious mechanical damages. A hot air anti-icing system is usually used to prevent the ice freezing on the surface. Hot air is bled from high pressure compressor which will lead to engine performance penalties. Some rotating parts such as fans of modern commercial aero-engine do not use hot air anti-icing system. Ice can be removed from fan surface by centrifugal and aerodynamic force that is larger than ice adhesive to the surface when the ice accumulates to a certain
thickness. In order to predict the ice shedding and fan aerodynamic characteristics, it is necessary to investigate the ice shape on fan surface. The type and shape of ice is affected by flight velocity, temperature, environment pressure, icing time and by meteorological parameters of liquid water content (LWC) and droplet diameter. The physical processes of ice accretion are complex and not completely understood. The physical description on icing and anti-icing such as the impingement of supercooled large droplets (SLD), the flow and heat transfer of runback water in glaze or mix icing, and the roughness effect on ice accretion were all developed constantly to improve the icing prediction in recent years1-6. Experimental and computational methods are used for simulating in-flight ice accretion and understanding the physics in icing. Some icing research tunnels are established and operated by NASA7, Boeing8, NRC9, Cox10, CIRA11, etc. Engine icing test cells for observing the ice shapes accreted on engine inlets and measuring the performance of the engine under icing conditions are also used in aero-engine companies. The cost of experimental study on the ice accretion is high due to the special requirement of facility to simulate the icing condition. Hence it is of the importance to develop numerical method and carry out numerical simulations of droplet trajectories, ice accretion and aerodynamic performance with ice on the aircraft surface. Many computational methods of the icing problem are developed to simulate the icing, anti-icing and de-icing process. Computational codes of icing have been developed at NASA (LEWICE12, 13, ANTICE14), ONERA (ONERA3D15), McGill University (FENSAP-ICE16), Boeing17, Ecole Polytechnique de Montreal (CANICE18), DAR19, et al. Though significant efforts have been devoted to the research of icing physics, the computational model of icing accretion still requires to be
