further developed. In general, the icing simulation consists of four basic steps20: computation of aerodynamic flow; computation of super-cooled water droplet trajectories and local collection efficiency; thermal balance analysis on the component wall; simulation of the ice shape. Flow field around the body can be obtained by solving Euler or Navier-Stokes equations and the convective heat transfer coefficient on the icing surface can be obtained at the same time. The local collection efficiency can be obtained satisfactory results using either Lagrangian or Eulerian approach. Most of the ice accretion codes use the Lagrangian approach to track the supercooled water droplet trajectories and subsequently the local collection efficiency on the surface can be calculated21-24. Compared with Lagrangian approach, Eulerian approach considers droplet phase as a continuous phase and uses the concept of droplet volume fraction to represent the amount of water within a given control volume. The air flow and water droplet equations are solved by using the same grid and numerical techniques in Eulerian approach. Eulerian approach is more convenient to determine the mass of water droplets that impinge on the surface25, 26. Most ice accretion and anti-icing codes compute the surface temperature and predict the ice growth rate using heat and mass balance model given by Messinger27. The heat balance model is important to predict the ice accretion processes. Wright et al.28 investigated the ice growth or geometry modification process and developed a robust scheme for ice growth calculation. Saeed et al.29 coupled a two dimensional CFD code (NSC2KE) and an ice accretion simulation code (CANICE) to analyze the heat transfer and temperature distribution on the surface of a multi-element airfoil with a hot air anti-icing system. Myers30 presented a mathematical model which considered the conduction of ice layer and used this improved model to simulate ice growth rates and freezing fractions. The physics behind icing process is not well understood and the models are improved continuously in order to solve this complex problem more accurately31-34. The computational studies of icing accretion on the stationary surfaces of aircraft have been carried out extensively. Numerical simulation of ice accretion on rotating components of aircraft engines is highly challenging because of the complex 3-D unsteady flow and the effects of rotation on droplet trajectories35. Lee and Loth36 studied the effect of liquid water droplet and ice accretion in 2D cascade and predicted the ice shape on a stage 67A stator blade. Dong et al.37 presented a Eulerian approach to calculate water impingement characteristics and a coupled heat and mass balance method to simulate the ice shape on the rotating cone of an aero-engine. Ice accretion on rotating blade of an aeroengine could adversely affect the fan performance and increase the vibration of the fan. It is necessary to predict the ice shape on the rotating blade ensuring the engine operation safety. The ice shape on the rotating blade is affected by icing time, flight parameters of velocity, pressure, temperature, and cloud parameters of liquid water content and water droplet diameter. This paper presents a numerical study on the icing process on the rotating blade. The flow field around the blade is
obtained using ANSYS FLUENT. The trajectories of supercooled water droplets and the collection efficiency are calculated by Eulerian approach. The effect of water droplets on the air flow is neglected due to the low liquid water content. Mass and energy balance on the surface of rotating blade is taken into account in icing process simulations. The NASA Rotor 67 blade is chosen as the computational model. The impingement and icing simulations are carried out under icing condition. The 3D icing accretion on Rotor 67 blade is predicted at design point. The ice shapes of accretion time of 5s, 10s and 15s are compared and analyzed. The simulation method can be used to predict the water collection rate, icing process and resulting change of aerodynamic shape for rotating blade. The icing simulation can also be used to understand the flow field behavior with accretion ice and analyze the associated performance penalties.
