MATHEMATICAL MODELS
Computation of Flow Field around the Blade
The flow field around the blade is obtained by using ANSYS FLUENT. The density based coupling algorithm is used to generate solutions for the air flow. The second order upwind advection scheme is employed for momentum and energy equations. The RNG k-ε turbulence model which considers swirling effects as well as the non-equilibrium wall functions is used to simulate the air flow turbulence around the blade.
Calculation of the Local Collection Efficiency
When the flow field of the air around the blade is calculated, the water impingement characteristics are calculated using Eulerian approach embedded in Ansys Fluent by User Defined Scalar (UDS) interface. Eulerian approach treats the droplets as a continuous phase and uses the concept of droplet volume fraction to represent the amount of water within a given control volume. The momentum and continuity equations for the droplets can be derived and solved in the same manner as the air flow equations. In most cases, the effects of water droplets on the air flow can be neglected due to the low LWC. The motion of water droplets in air flow field subjects to fluid drag, buoyancy and gravity forces acting on the water droplets. For small supercooled water droplets, the effects of buoyancy and gravity forces is small comparing with the aerodynamic forces and can be ignored. The governing equations of the water droplet phase movement are described as follows:
