Thermodynamic Model of Ice Accretion
In most cases, thermal analysis should be done in ice accretion prediction. Several heat flux such as convective heat transfer, sensible heating of the supercooled water impinging on the surface, the kinetic energy of water droplets, the evaporation heat transfer, the latent heat of condensation of water freezing are usually considered in thermal balance analysis. In some case, especially in glaze ice simulation, mass balance of water should also be taken into account because the impinging water would run back the surface due to aerodynamic force. Fig.1 illustrates the mass flow rates and heat flux through a control volume on the blade surface. The heat transfer between air and water droplets should also be taken into account because air temperature would increase as it flow through the passage of fan blades. When blade surface temperature is higher than icing point, no ice would deposit on the blade surface.
