RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Computation Model and Case Setup
The NASA Rotor 67 blade is chosen as the computational model. It is the first stage rotor of a high-speed low aspect ratio two-stage axial flow fan with a design pressure ratio of 1.63 and a mass flow rate of 33.25 kg/s at inlet total temperature of 288K. The total pressure at the inlet is 1atm, and the blade rotating speed is 16043 RPM41. A pair of periodic boundary is applied to reduce the mesh size and boundary layer meshes are applied. The computational domain and mesh is shown in Fig.3, which has approximate
220,000 hexahedral elements. A pressure-inlet and a pressureoutlet boundary are specified at the flow inlet and outlet respectively. The moving reference frame is used to simulate the flow field with the blade rotating. The boundaries are specified with the data of design point parameters. The temperature of air flow is 253.15K and the LWC is 0.5g/m3 in icing simulation. The mean volume diameter (MVD) is set to 20 μm.
Fig.3 The computational mesh
When flow field around blade is obtained, droplet impingement property can be calculated by Eulerian approach which solves droplet movement governing equations using the same computational mesh as that of air flow computation. The equations of water phase are discrete under absolute frame. The water phase velocity at the flow inlet is set as the same as that of air flow and the volume fraction of water are set to be unit. Boundary conditions downstream the inlet flow are not required to be specified due to no diffusion terms in the water phase governing equations.
Droplet Impingement Property
Water droplets trajectories in relative frame of reference are shown in Fig.4. The local droplet collection efficiency distributions on the blade pressure surface and suction surface are shown in Fig.5 and Fig.6. The collection efficiency on the blade pressure surface is greater than that on the blade suction surface. Most of water droplets impinge on the blade pressure surface due to the incidence angle of water droplets at design point.
