CONCLUSION
Numerical simulation of ice accretion on rotating components of aircraft engines is still a challenge due to the complex 3-D unsteady flow and the effects of rotation. The ice shapes on the rotating blade are affected by icing time, flight parameters of velocity, pressure, temperature, and cloud parameters of liquid water content and water droplet diameter. This paper presents a numerical study on the icing process on the rotating blade. The flow field around the blade is obtained using ANSYS FLUENT. The trajectories of supercooled water droplets and the collection efficiency are calculated by Eulerian approach. The 3D computation of water droplets impingement and icing accretion on Rotor 67 blade is predicted at design rotating speed. The computation results show that the collection efficiency on the blade pressure surface is greater than that on the blade suction surface. Most of water droplets impinge on the blade pressure surface due to the incidence angle of water droplets at design point. The value of local collection efficiency at the leading edge is highest. The collection efficiency increases along the radial direction at the leading edge due to different rotating radius and the collection efficiency at the leading edge could be exceeding to unit. The collection efficiency at the pressure side decreases along the radial direction from top to bottom and increases along axial flow direction due to the blade geometry. The ice shapes of accretion time of 5s, 10s and 15s are simulated and the ice shapes at different span positions of the rotating blade are compared. Ice accumulates quickly at the blade leading edge due to high collection efficiency. The simulation results also show that the predicted ice shapes are different at different span positions. More ice tends to accumulate on the pressure surface at the blade root.
The method presented in this paper can be used to predict the water collection rate, icing process and resulting change of aerodynamic shape. The icing simulation results can also be used to analyze the performance penalties of rotor blade due to icing.
